# Line endings are normalized to LF in the repository AND in the working tree on
# every platform.
#
# Without this file, a Windows checkout converts text files to CRLF (git's
# core.autocrlf default there), and any code that matches on exact byte
# sequences reads a different file than the one that was committed. That is not
# hypothetical here: the workflow drift guard in tests/hardening.rs matches
# "\n  <job>:\n" to locate a YAML job, and it went red on the windows-latest CI
# leg for v0.108.0 — a failure with no defect behind it, costing a full
# push/CI/fix round on an otherwise green release. The test now normalizes CRLF
# itself; this file removes the class instead of the instance, for every future
# fixture, snapshot, and exact-match assertion.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is what pins the WORKING TREE, not only the
# blob. `text=auto` alone still hands Windows CRLF on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats: never touch the bytes. `text=auto` heuristically detects most
# of these, but naming them makes the intent explicit and removes the guess for
# the ones with text-like headers.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.tgz    binary
*.xz     binary
*.zst    binary
*.wasm   binary
*.so     binary
*.dylib  binary
*.dll    binary
*.exe    binary
*.a      binary
*.safetensors binary
*.onnx   binary
*.bin    binary
*.db     binary
*.sqlite binary

# Windows-native scripts are the one text class that genuinely needs CRLF:
# cmd.exe mis-parses an LF-only .bat/.cmd (a trailing `goto :eof` on the last
# line without CR can be read as `goto :eof\n`), so pin them the other way.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf

# Vendored C sources ship as-is — sqlite-vec is built from vendor/ by build.rs
# and its upstream checksums must keep matching.
vendor/** -text
